# static/dashboard is BUILD OUTPUT, and git must never try to merge it.
#
# Vite content-hashes the emitted names, so two branches that both rebuild the
# dashboard write DIFFERENT paths for the same chunk. Git pairs those as two
# renames of one base blob and three-way merges them — 979 KB of minified
# JavaScript, on a line-based algorithm. Measured on this tree, one such merge
# splices nine conflict-marker lines into the committed artefacts: three into
# each side's entry bundle and three into index.html. Both bundles then fail
# `node --check`. Anyone who resolves by staging rather than rebuilding
# commits a dashboard that does not parse.
#
# `-merge` is git's binary-merge behaviour: the conflict still happens — the
# same four paths come back unmerged either way — and git leaves OURS in the
# working tree instead of writing markers into it. THE RESOLUTION IS THE ONLY
# ONE THAT IS EVER CORRECT for generated files: rebuild, do not merge.
#
#     make dashboard && git add -A -- static/dashboard
#
# NOT `binary` and NOT `-diff`, which would also be tempting: `make
# dashboard-check` is `npm run build` followed by `git diff --exit-code --
# static/dashboard`, and ci.yml prints that patch under "The diff above is what
# a rebuild produced". Under `binary` it becomes "Binary files … differ", and
# `git log -G` over the tree drops from 66 matching commits to none. `-merge`
# costs neither: the diff is byte-identical to having no attribute at all.
#
# `merge=binary` is an exact synonym and says less. A merge DRIVER is the wrong
# mechanism entirely — `merge.<name>.driver` is git config, cannot be committed,
# and degrades silently to the marker-writing text merge in every clone where
# nobody ran `git config`.
#
# The whole emitted tree, not just assets/: index.html is generated too and
# took the third marker injection, and protocol.js is the second build target.
# Not `static/**` — static/crewlet-icon.svg is a hand-maintained embed the
# build does not write.
#
# static/merge_test.go asserts the EFFECT of this line, via `git check-attr`,
# because a rule held by prose alone decays (adr/README) — and because this
# file is one directive needing a paragraph beside it, so the likeliest way to
# lose it is a `#` in front of it, which no substring check over these bytes
# would notice.
static/dashboard/** -merge
